    "description": "Interprovincial migration redistributes residents across Iran’s provincial boundaries. Research connects its geography with unequal opportunities and environmental conditions, while showing why net migration alone cannot describe all movement.",
    "tags": ["Internal migration", "Provinces", "Regional inequality", "Environmental change", "Population redistribution"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T04:06:45Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T04:06:45Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Interprovincial Migration in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand migration across Iran’s provincial boundaries, regional inequalities, environmental research, and census limitations."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "boundaries-and-balances",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Crossing a provincial boundary",
        "content": "Interprovincial migration classifies a residential move by its administrative geography. It differs from rural-to-urban migration, which classifies the settlements at either end. A move can belong to both categories, but crossing a provincial boundary does not necessarily mean leaving a village for a city. Iranian migration research distinguishes recent migration, previous residence, and lifetime migration; these measures answer different questions and should not be combined without checking their definitions. [s1]\n\nTwo further distinctions help readers interpret provincial comparisons. In-migration counts arrivals and outmigration counts departures. Net migration subtracts departures from arrivals, whereas gross movement includes both directions. A small net balance can therefore conceal considerable movement. The national synthesis by Sadeghi and colleagues describes limited population redistribution despite ongoing mobility, illustrating why migration activity and its net demographic effect require separate attention. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "regional-pattern",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A geography of unequal opportunities",
        "content": "The published abstract of that synthesis identifies net population gains in central provinces around Tehran and losses toward eastern and western peripheral areas. It links this geography to socioeconomic inequalities. This is a broad interpretation of census-era patterns, rather than a claim that every province within those regions behaves identically or that the same destinations remain dominant today. [s1]\n\nFor a reader comparing places, a provincial balance is best treated as an entry point. It does not identify every origin–destination connection, distinguish household members’ individual motives, or establish whether a move was permanent. Those questions require more detailed migration histories than an aggregate balance provides.",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "environment-and-method",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Environmental pressures alongside economic conditions",
        "content": "Farzanegan, Gholipour, and Javadian examined migration and air pollution using census observations and satellite measurements. Their models associate greater aerosol pollution with higher net outmigration and stronger economic activity with lower net outmigration. The study supports considering environmental quality alongside economic opportunity; it does not establish pollution as the motive of every household that moved. [s2]\n\nIts geography needs particular care. The dependent variable uses departures minus arrivals in selected major cities within each province, divided by their combined population. That is a net-outmigration measure, whose positive direction differs from a conventional net-inmigration balance. It is also not a complete census of all moves between provincial boundaries. Provincial labels on a research result must therefore be read together with the actual population covered. [s2]\n\nThe practical lesson is to ask what a map measures before interpreting its colours: arrivals, departures, a net balance, or a population-normalized rate. Evidence for an environmental relationship can enrich the explanation of regional migration without replacing those basic distinctions. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      }
    ],
