    "description": "Skilled migration from Iran includes the international movement of researchers, professionals, and other workers with specialized education or expertise. Its significance depends on how skills are defined, whether departures are temporary or lasting, and what connections migrants maintain with Iran.",
    "tags": ["Skilled migration", "Human capital", "Researchers", "Professional mobility", "Diaspora"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T04:06:45Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T04:06:45Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Skilled Migration from Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore skilled migration from Iran, the evidence on professional and scientific mobility, and the limits of brain-drain estimates."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "defining-skills",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Who counts as a skilled migrant?",
        "content": "Skilled migration is not a single statistical category. Education-based accounts identify degree holders; professional accounts focus on occupations; scientific-mobility studies track researchers. These approaches overlap without describing identical populations. OECD country profiles, for example, distinguish educational attainment and highly educated emigration, while scientific-mobility research uses publication affiliations. A figure about researchers cannot automatically stand for all skilled workers. [s1][s4]\n\nThe distinction between a stock and a flow is equally important. A population of skilled people living abroad accumulates over time; it is not the number leaving in a particular year. Nor does an overseas affiliation necessarily establish permanent settlement. These distinctions are essential when reading research on Iran’s internationally mobile professionals. [s2][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "drivers-and-interpretation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Careers, institutions, and the decision to leave",
        "content": "The Stanford Iran 2040 working paper interprets skilled emigration through interacting economic, political, and social conditions. Its framework distinguishes long-running pressures from particular triggers and from networks that help people move. This is an analytical account, not a survey establishing one motive shared by every emigrant. [s2]\n\nA Migration Policy Institute article likewise discusses research opportunities, career prospects, restrictions affecting academics, sanctions, and economic deterioration. It also includes health professionals in the discussion, showing why the subject extends beyond universities. These accounts describe the circumstances examined at publication; they do not provide a verified inventory of professional departures near the editorial cutoff. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "scientific-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What scientific mobility can reveal",
        "content": "The Stanford study combines migration statistics with an algorithm identifying scholars of Iranian descent in publication records. Its scholar population should therefore be distinguished from a register of Iranian-born people who recently departed. Descent, birthplace, employment location, and migration date are different attributes. [s2]\n\nA separate study of scientific mobility in the Middle East and North Africa uses affiliations recorded in publications to examine movement and international collaboration. This approach makes cross-border research connections visible, but its evidence concerns publishing scholars. It cannot establish the movements of professionals who do not publish, and a collaboration link should not be interpreted as a residential move. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "consequences",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Loss of expertise and continuing connections",
        "content": "“Brain drain” describes concern about the loss of expertise available to institutions and employers at home. The Stanford paper also discusses potential diaspora contributions, including collaboration, investment, and temporary or lasting return. These are possible channels of contribution rather than proof that departures have been offset. [s2]\n\nAn assessment of Iran therefore needs several questions rather than one headline total: which skills are leaving, which institutions face shortages, how long migrants remain abroad, and what professional connections continue. Existing research establishes the importance of the subject, while differences in definitions limit comparisons between estimates. A defensible account keeps those populations and measurement methods visible. [s1][s2][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s4"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
